Hi Angus,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. It is desktop software for adapting
downloaded 3D models and constructing printable parts from dimensions,
sketches and tested components. I know Maker’s Muse does not promote AI
companies, so I want to be clear up front: this is a 3D-design software review
offer, not an AI product pitch.
Solidon3D is fully usable without AI; its optional chat is only another way to
call the same fixed modelling tools as the menus.

The review question is practical: can a beginner fix a downloaded STL without
rebuilding it in CAD?

In the 0.3.0 build, Solidon recognises an existing hole in an imported mesh,
keeps its position and axis, and changes the diameter as a reversible history
step. The user can then add a standards-based heat-set insert or screw
clearance, with tolerances taken from a small calibration piece printed on
their own machine.

Since the first public 0.1 demo, 220 curated user-visible changes have added
in-viewport sketching, reusable parametric parts made from history without
code, printable matching nuts and screws, bearing seats, per-spool process
data in 3MF, and Windows, macOS and Linux support. It is deliberately not a
full CAD replacement or a slicer; it targets the frustrating gap between
“model downloaded” and “part fits.”

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/

I can provide a no-strings review licence, the 0.3.0 build, deliberately flawed
STL, expected measurements and comparison prints. No payment, no approval
request and no expectation of a positive result. If the challenge interests
you, reply “review file” and I will send the complete pack in one link.

